Q: CSS Anchoring Text to Image Corner A: <p>The issue is that your <strong>image is centered using <code>margin: auto</code></strong>, but your <strong>title (<code>h3</code>) is left-aligned</strong> with a fixed <code>margin-left</code>. So when the image resizes, the title stays stuck to the page margin instead of following the image.</p>
<p>A clean solution is to <strong>wrap the title and image inside a centered container</strong> and align the title left relative to that container, not the page. Example CSS:</p>
<pre><code>.card {
    background-color: var(--bg-color);
    padding: 1vw;
    margin-top: 1vw;
    width: fit-content;   /* container shrinks to content */
    max-width: 95vw;      /* never exceed viewport */
    margin-left: auto;    /* center container */
    margin-right: auto;
    border-style: none;
}

.card h3 {
    color: var(--text-color);
    font-family: 'Lato', sans-serif;
    font-size: 3vw;
    text-align: left;     /* aligned to container */
    margin: 0 0 0.5vw 0;
}

.card img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;          /* scales to container width */
    height: auto;
    max-height: 75vh;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Key points:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>.card</code> container is centered and responsive.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>h3</code> is left-aligned relative to the container.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Image scales with container width and respects viewport height.</p>
</li>
<li><p>No fixed <code>margin-left</code> on <code>h3</code> → prevents mismatch when viewport changes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This approach ensures that your <strong>title stays aligned with the centered image</strong> regardless of viewport size.</p>

Q: Can i hide font color in delphi&#39;s object inspector? A: <p>Unfortunately <code>UnpublishedProperty</code> works only for direct properties, but not for sub properties of those.</p>
<p>A strategy to accomplish your goal would be to create a wrapper class for <code>TFont</code> publishing all the <code>TFont</code> properties you want omitting the <code>Color</code> property.</p>
<pre><code>  TMyFontWrapper = class(TPersistent)
  private
    FFont: TFont;
    function GetCharset: TFontCharset;
    function GetHeight: Integer;
    function GetName: TFontName;
    function GetOrientation: Integer;
    function GetPitch: TFontPitch;
    function GetQuality: TFontQuality;
    function GetSize: Integer;
    function GetStyle: TFontStyles;
    procedure SetCharset(const Value: TFontCharset);
    procedure SetHeight(const Value: Integer);
    procedure SetName(const Value: TFontName);
    procedure SetOrientation(const Value: Integer);
    procedure SetPitch(const Value: TFontPitch);
    procedure SetQuality(const Value: TFontQuality);
    procedure SetSize(const Value: Integer);
    procedure SetStyle(const Value: TFontStyles);
  public
    property Font: TFont read FFont write FFont;
  published
    property Charset: TFontCharset read GetCharset write SetCharset;
    property Height: Integer read GetHeight write SetHeight;
    property Name: TFontName read GetName write SetName;
    property Orientation: Integer read GetOrientation write SetOrientation default 0;
    property Pitch: TFontPitch read GetPitch write SetPitch default fpDefault;
    property Size: Integer read GetSize write SetSize stored False;
    property Style: TFontStyles read GetStyle write SetStyle;
    property Quality: TFontQuality read GetQuality write SetQuality default fqDefault;
  end;
</code></pre>
<p>For simplicity this is just the implementation for <code>CharSet</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>function TMyFontWrapper.GetCharset: TFontCharset;
begin
  Result := Font.Charset;
end;

procedure TMyFontWrapper.SetCharset(const Value: TFontCharset);
begin
  Font.Charset := Value;
end;
</code></pre>
<p>Your <code>TSvhProgressbar</code> should look like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>type
  TSvhProgressbar= class(TCustomControl)
  private
    FFontWrapper: TMyFontWrapper;
    procedure SetFontWrapper(const Value: TMyFontWrapper);
    // other code
  public
    constructor Create(AOwner: TComponent); override;
    destructor Destroy; override;
    // other code
  published
    // other code
    property Font: TMyFontWrapper read FFontWrapper write SetFontWrapper;
  end;

constructor TSvhProgressbar.Create(AOwner: TComponent);
begin
  inherited;
  // other code
  FFontWrapper := TMyFontWrapper.Create();
  FFontWrapper.Font := inherited Font;
end;

destructor TSvhProgressbar.Destroy;
begin
  FFontWrapper.Free;
  // other code
  inherited;
end;

procedure TSvhProgressbar.SetFontWrapper(const Value: TMyFontWrapper);
begin
  inherited Font := Value.Font;
end;
</code></pre>
<p>A drawback is that you lose the <code>TFont</code> dialog, but that would bring the <code>Color</code> property back anyway. Of course you can make your own font wrapper property editor. See <em>ToolsAPI\VCLEditors.pas</em> for some hints for that.</p>
<p>Note, that I didn't find the time to test this, so there might still exist some glitches or hidden problems.</p>

Q: Returned from Postgres timstamptz always in UTC A: <p>PostgreSQL does not preserve the time zone information of a <code>timestamp with time zone</code>; internally, a timestamp is stored as the number of microseconds since 2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. When you query a <code>timestamp with time zone</code>, PostgreSQL renders the timestamp in the current session time zone. With that JDBC driver, that is the time zone of the JVM.</p>
<p>Here is a sample JDBC program that sets the JVM time zone, which sets the session time zone for the database session established afterwards, and retrieves the current timestamp, which is of type <code>timestamp with time zone</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class Timestamp {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws ClassNotFoundException, java.sql.SQLException {
        Class.forName(&quot;org.postgresql.Driver&quot;);

        System.setProperty(&quot;user.timezone&quot;, &quot;Asia/Kolkata&quot;);

        java.sql.Connection conn = java.sql.DriverManager.getConnection(&quot;jdbc:postgresql://127.0.0.1:5432/test?user=laurenz&amp;password=apassword0&quot;);

        java.sql.ResultSet rs = conn.createStatement().executeQuery(&quot;SELECT current_timestamp&quot;);

        rs.next();
        System.out.println(rs.getString(1));

        rs.close();
        conn.close();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>For me right now, this prints</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>2025-12-30 20:29:40.703866+05:30
</code></pre>
<p>An alternative is to set the time zone in an already established database session by running the SQL statement</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>SET timezone = 'Europe/Vienna'
</code></pre>
